<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    S2, the routing ceiling. plans/0010-s2-routing.md.

    Throwaway. Task R7 deletes this directory and records the deleting commit's parent in
    docs/spike-results.md, exactly as plans/0004 does for S4.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <RootNamespace>S2.Routing</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      plans/0010: "references the arithmetic substrate and nothing else of ours", and "It must not
      reference Borough.Core beyond the substrate."

      Compile-linking the three substrate files rather than taking a ProjectReference on
      Borough.Core is what makes that sentence mechanical instead of aspirational. Under a project
      reference "beyond the substrate" is a rule nothing checks and every table, every quantity and
      Simulation itself become nameable from here; under this, they are not present in the
      compilation at all. The spike can name Fixed, IntegerMath and Transcendental and can name
      nothing else of ours, and the day somebody reaches for a Core type they get a build error
      rather than a habit.

      The substrate is self-contained — Arithmetic/*.cs imports System.Numerics and nothing of
      ours — so this pulls exactly the substrate and no transitive tail.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\..\src\Borough.Core\Arithmetic\*.cs"
             Link="Substrate\%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The analysers are loaded here deliberately, and it is the substrate decision's other half.

      plans/0010's prerequisites are explicit that the spike "may not use floating point", for the
      stated reason that a router computing travel times in double measures a thing the simulation
      will never run. That rule was previously carried by discipline; BOR0201 carries it as a build
      error. Every other diagnostic comes along with it and every one of them is wanted in Graph/
      and Routing/ — that code is a model of arithmetic the simulation will actually perform, so
      raw division, Math.*, masked shifts and System.Random are hazards here for the same reasons
      they are hazards in Core.

      Harness/ is exempted from three of them, narrowly and by path, in .editorconfig — with the
      argument written there rather than here.

      Same form as Borough.Core's own reference: a build-time input, never a dependency. Nothing in
      Borough.Analysers is nameable from this project.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Borough.Analysers\Borough.Analysers.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
